This repository maintains a patch set that enables GPU-accelerated GGUF
inference in Ollama on Intel Macs with discrete AMD (Metal 3) GPUs, plus
multi-GPU (multi-die) support. It is NOT a fork of Ollama or llama.cpp; it is a
thin, re-appliable overlay on top of pinned upstream versions of both.

## Why this project exists

Upstream Ollama runs GGUF models CPU-only on Intel Macs with AMD GPUs:
- Ollama's macOS GPU story moved to MLX (Apple Silicon, unified memory) in
  v0.19. The llama.cpp/ggml Metal backend it still uses for GGUF is gated to
  arm64; on x86_64 it is compiled out, so inference falls back to CPU.
- Stock llama.cpp Metal also runs terribly on these GPUs because its matmul
  kernels assume Apple-GPU `simdgroup_matrix` intrinsics (`has_simdgroup_mm`),
  which AMD RDNA2 lacks. Measured ~2 t/s on a W6800X die (slower than CPU).

The `Basten7/iRon-Llama` fork solved the kernel problem: it forces
`has_simdgroup_mm = false` and ships threadgroup-tiled `mul_mm` GEMM kernels
that run well on AMD. On this exact hardware it reaches ~339 t/s prompt /
~87 t/s generation on ONE die, with coherent output - roughly 3x the CPU. This
project ports that kernel work onto the llama.cpp version Ollama pins, wires it
into Ollama's build for x86_64, teaches Ollama's discovery/scheduler about the
AMD dies, and adds multi-die support.

## Target hardware / platform (the only supported config)

- Machine: Apple Mac Pro 2019 (MacPro7,1), Intel Xeon W (x86_64).
- GPUs: 2x AMD Radeon PRO W6800X Duo = 4 GPU dies, ~32 GB usable each
  (`recommendedMaxWorkingSetSize` ~= 34 GB/die), non-UMA (discrete VRAM/PCIe).
- GPU caps (from ggml device init): `simdgroup reduction = true`,
  `simdgroup matrix mul. = false`, `has unified memory = false`,
  family `MTLGPUFamilyMetal3 (5001)`.
- OS: macOS 26.x. Toolchain: Xcode + Metal; Go (MacPorts) >= the version in
  Ollama's `go.mod`; MacPorts cmake/ninja/git. No Homebrew on this host.

## Version pins (the heart of the repatching strategy)

Two files at the repo root pin exactly what we patch against:
- `LLAMA_CPP_VERSION`  - the upstream llama.cpp tag Ollama fetches (currently
  `b10353`). This is the tree our kernel patch applies to.
- `OLLAMA_VERSION`     - the Ollama tag + commit we integrate with (currently
  `v0.32.9` / `1d5febee...`).

These MUST mirror the pins inside the matching Ollama release:
- Ollama's own `LLAMA_CPP_VERSION` file is the source of truth for the
  llama.cpp tag. On every Ollama bump, re-read it and update ours.

## How Ollama builds the GGML backend (integration point)

`ollama/llama/server/CMakeLists.txt`:
- Reads `LLAMA_CPP_VERSION`, then `FetchContent_Declare(llama_cpp ...)` clones
  `ggml-org/llama.cpp` at that tag and applies an "Ollama-compat" patch
  (`ollama/llama/compat/`) that shims published GGUF blobs.
- Env override `OLLAMA_LLAMA_CPP_SOURCE=<dir>` makes Ollama build against a
  LOCAL llama.cpp tree AND skips the compat patch. This is our injection point:
  we point Ollama at a patched local checkout of `b10353`.

amd64 macOS build entrypoint: `ollama/scripts/build_darwin.sh` (the
`ARCH=amd64` branch, `CMAKE_ARCH=x86_64`). It currently builds `ggml-cpu` +
MLX; our patch also builds and installs the Metal backend for x86_64.

## Repatching strategy (keep changes isolated and cheap to re-apply)

Golden rule: NEVER hand-edit a vendored Ollama or llama.cpp checkout in place
and call it done. All changes live HERE as:
1. `patches/*.patch` - git-format patches applied to a clean upstream checkout.
   - `patches/llama-cpp/` applies to llama.cpp @ `LLAMA_CPP_VERSION`
     (AMD-Metal kernels, build un-gating, multi-device backend registration).
   - `patches/ollama/` applies to Ollama @ `OLLAMA_VERSION` (discovery / VRAM
     reporting / scheduler placement for the AMD dies, build_darwin.sh).
2. `reference/iron-llama-b6123/` - the proven upstream kernels (MIT, from
   `Basten7/iRon-Llama`, base llama.cpp `b6123`) kept verbatim for provenance
   and as the semantic source when porting forward to `b10353`.
3. `scripts/` - deterministic clone -> patch -> build -> bench automation.

Bumping versions (the routine maintenance task):
- Update `OLLAMA_VERSION`; read the new Ollama `LLAMA_CPP_VERSION`; update ours.
- `git apply --3way` the patches onto the new trees; resolve rejects.
- Because the kernels are version-sensitive, prefer re-porting semantically
  (force `has_simdgroup_mm=false`; enable threadgroup `mul_mm`/`mul_mm_id` when
  `!has_unified_memory`; keep `flash_attn_ext` off on AMD) over blind apply.
- Always keep the standalone llama.cpp `llama-server` build as a fallback
  inference path (it already works at ~87 t/s and depends only on llama.cpp).

## The kernel change, in one paragraph (so ports stay faithful)

In `ggml-metal.m` device init: report `has_simdgroup_reduction` for
`MTLGPUFamilyMetal3`/`Mac2`, force `ctx->has_simdgroup_mm = false`, and gate
the matmul kernel set on `allow_mm_kernels = has_simdgroup_mm ||
!has_unified_memory` so the threadgroup-tiled `mul_mm`/`mul_mm_id` kernels load
on non-UMA AMD. Disable `flash_attn_ext` when `!has_unified_memory ||
!has_simdgroup_mm`. Device selection honors `MTLCopyAllDevices()` +
`GGML_METAL_DEVICE_INDEX`. The `.metal` file carries the ~1200-line tiled GEMM
kernels. Multi-die work replaces the single global device context with one
context per registered `ggml_backend_device`.

## Build / test workflow

- `scripts/env.sh`     - sets pins, locates Go (MacPorts), Xcode, work dirs.
- `scripts/bootstrap.sh` - clones Ollama @ `OLLAMA_VERSION` and llama.cpp @
  `LLAMA_CPP_VERSION` into `work/` (git-ignored).
- `scripts/apply-patch.sh` - applies `patches/` onto the `work/` checkouts.
- `scripts/build.sh`   - builds Ollama against the patched tree via
  `OLLAMA_LLAMA_CPP_SOURCE=work/llama.cpp`.
- `scripts/bench.sh`   - runs llama-bench / `ollama run` timing harness.

Validation gates (Metal on AMD is correctness-fragile - always check both):
1. CORRECTNESS: a short prompt must produce coherent text (no `@@@@`/NaN).
2. SPEED: tokens/sec must clearly beat the CPU baseline (~30 t/s tg on 3B).
Record results in `bench/` with model, quant, die index, t/s, and build pin.

Useful env knobs (defaults found best on this hardware):
- `GGML_METAL_DEVICE_INDEX=<0..3>` selects the die.
- `GGML_METAL_N_CB=4` and small batches (`-ub 32 -b 32`) help non-UMA.

## Scope / non-goals

- No tensor-parallel speedup: not feasible on non-UMA Metal. Multi-die is for
  CAPACITY (run >32 GB models via layer split) and CHEAP THROUGHPUT (one model
  per die / concurrent serving), not higher single-stream tokens/sec.
- Do not vendor full Ollama or llama.cpp source trees into this repo; clone at
  build time via the pins. Keep the repo to patches + scripts + docs + the
  small reference kernels.
- Do not run `sudo`/package installs from automation; surface the command for
  the user to run (this host gates `sudo` behind a password).

## Conventions

- Shell scripts: `bash`, `set -euo pipefail`, POSIX-ish, no Homebrew assumptions.
- Patches: `git format-patch` style, one logical change per file, named
  `NN-short-description.patch`, grouped under `patches/llama-cpp/` or
  `patches/ollama/`.
- Docs live in `docs/`; curated benchmark results in `bench/*.md`.
- Keep `LLAMA_CPP_VERSION` / `OLLAMA_VERSION` as the single source of truth for
  pins; scripts read them, never hardcode tags.
